// std.rt_float: the two float printers, `print_f32` and `print_f64`.
// D18.1
//
// They stand here and not in std.rt because a compiler that builds this module
// must accept floats, and the C bootstrap does not: stage1 refuses a float
// type and a float literal outright, so no program it builds can reach a float
// printer and it never loads this module. stage2 accepts floats, loads this
// module as a root of every closure exactly as whole-program compilation loads
// std.rt, and emits a call here for each float the print family is given
// (toolchain.md 6 item 19).
// D18.1, D9.10, D12.2
//
// The module imports std.libc, std.rt and std.strbuf and nothing else. It
// writes its bytes through rt.emit, so a float shares the buffer of its
// descriptor with every other value printed beside it; std.strbuf is there for
// append_f64 and append_f32, which give the same bytes to a buffer
// (stdlib.md 2.12).
// D11.5
//
// What it produces. The digits are the shortest decimal that reads back as the
// value in the value's own type and, among the texts of that length that read
// back, the one nearest the value, ties to the even last digit. The layout
// around them: exponent form when the decimal exponent of the leading digit is
// below -4 or at 17 and above, fixed form between, `.0` appended when a fixed
// text has neither a point nor an exponent, the exponent written as `e`, a
// sign and at least two digits, `-0.0` for a negative zero, and `nan` for
// every NaN whatever its sign bit, since the print family lists `inf`, `-inf`
// and `nan` and no `-nan`.
// D18.2, D18.3, D11.7
//
// How it produces them, which the shortest-digit rule leaves open and
// toolchain.md 5.1 describes: snprintf is asked for one significant digit,
// then two, and so on, and the first length whose text strtod reads back as
// the value wins. 17 digits for an f64 and 9 for an f32 always read back, so
// the search ends. snprintf returns the nearest decimal of the length asked
// for, which is not always the one to keep: for a normal power of two above
// the least normal the values that read back reach half an ulp above the value
// and only a quarter below, the binade below being coarser, so the nearest
// decimal can fall short of that interval while the next one up falls inside
// it. That neighbour is tried before the length grows. The neighbour below
// never needs trying, since the gap below a float is never wider than the gap
// above.
// D18.2
//
// This is the one place in the runtime that asks the C library for a
// formatted value. It asks two things of it which the C standard permits but
// does not require and glibc provides: a correctly rounded printf, ties to
// even, and a correctly rounded strtod down to the subnormals. strtod reports
// ERANGE there, which is ignored, since only the value it returns matters.
//
// Read this paragraph before you change a buffer or a loop bound, for the
// reason std.rt states at its own: a check inside a printing path would call
// the failure path, which prints. The bounds here are exact and the arithmetic
// has no slack. A decimal holds 17 digits, which is every digit an f64 needs
// and eight more than an f32 needs. TEXT_MAX is 40 and the longest text is 24
// bytes: a sign, a digit, a point, sixteen digits and `e-308`. SCRATCH_MAX is
// 32 and the longest text snprintf writes into it is `%.16e` of a finite
// double, which is 23 bytes and a NUL. EXPONENT_MAX is 8 and an exponent is
// at most three digits, since 308 and 324 are the widest an f64 reaches.
import std.libc;
import std.rt;
import std.strbuf;

/// The significant digits an f64 and an f32 need at most: 17 and 9 always read
/// back, which is what ends the search.
/// D18.2
u64 DIGITS_F64 = 17;
u64 DIGITS_F32 = 9;

/// The decimal exponent of the leading digit at which the form changes: below
/// the first is exponent form, at the second and above is exponent form,
/// between them is fixed form.
/// D18.3
i32 EXP_LOW = -4;
i32 EXP_HIGH = 17;

/// The least number of exponent digits the print family writes, so 1e5 prints
/// `1e+05`.
/// D11.7
u64 EXP_MIN_DIGITS = 2;

/// The bytes each buffer holds; the paragraph above states why each is enough.
u64 TEXT_MAX = 40;
u64 TEXT_MAX_NUL = 41;
u64 SCRATCH_MAX = 32;
u64 EXPONENT_MAX = 8;

u64 DECIMAL_BASE = 10;

/// The largest finite value of each type, which is what tells a finite value
/// from an infinity: a comparison is false for a NaN either way, so the NaN is
/// taken first.
/// D6.12
f64 F64_MAX = 1.7976931348623157e308;
f32 F32_MAX = 3.4028234663852886e38;

/// The bit that carries the sign of a zero, since -0.0 == 0.0 and no comparison
/// can tell the two apart.
/// D6.12, D18.3
u64 F64_SIGN_SHIFT = 63;
u32 F32_SIGN_SHIFT = 31;

/// The bytes of an f64 and of an f32, which the sign test copies.
u64 F64_BYTES = 8;
u64 F32_BYTES = 4;

/// The format snprintf is asked for: a decimal with `precision` digits after
/// the point, in exponent form. `%.*e` takes the precision as an argument, so
/// one text serves every length.
string FORMAT = "%.*e";

/// The C library the search needs. They are declared here rather than in
/// std.libc because a float in a signature is a float in a module, and std.libc
/// rides into every closure behind std.rt, stage1's included; this module is
/// the one stage1 never loads. A program that declares strtod or strtof itself
/// must spell it the same way.
/// D9.10, D9.8
extern fn i32 snprintf(char mut* buf, u64 size, char* fmt, i32 precision, f64 v);
extern fn f64 strtod(char* s, void* end);
extern fn f32 strtof(char* s, void* end);

/// One float as a decimal: the significant digits of the shortest text that
/// reads back as the value, most significant first, and the power of ten the
/// first of them stands for, so the value is digit[0].digit[1..] times ten to
/// the exponent. The sign is kept apart, since -0.0 has no digit to carry it.
struct decimal {
    u64 digit_count;
    i32 exponent;
    u8[DIGITS_F64] digit;
}

// ---- reading snprintf's text ---------------------------------------------------------

fn bool is_digit(char c) {
    return c >= '0' && c <= '9';
}

fn u64 digit_of(char c) {
    return cast(c, u64) - cast('0', u64);
}

fn char char_of(u64 digit) {
    return cast(cast('0', u64) + digit, char);
}

/// Reads printf's %e text back into digits and an exponent. The text is
/// d.ddde+xx, without the point at precision 0, and never carries a sign here:
/// only the magnitude is printed. A text with no digit at all, which is what a
/// failed printf leaves behind, scans as the decimal zero, so every caller gets
/// a decimal it can lay out.
fn void scan(char* s, decimal mut* d) {
    char@ text = s[0..libc.strlen(s)];
    d->digit_count = 0;
    d->exponent = 0;
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < text.len && text[i] != 'e') {
        if (is_digit(text[i]) && d->digit_count < DIGITS_F64) {
            d->digit[d->digit_count] = cast(digit_of(text[i]), u8);
            d->digit_count += 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if (d->digit_count == 0) {
        d->digit[0] = 0;
        d->digit_count = 1;
    }
    if (i >= text.len) {
        return;
    }
    // The `e` and what follows it.
    i += 1;
    bool mut negative = false;
    if (i < text.len && (text[i] == '+' || text[i] == '-')) {
        negative = text[i] == '-';
        i += 1;
    }
    i32 mut exponent = 0;
    while (i < text.len && is_digit(text[i])) {
        exponent = exponent * cast(DECIMAL_BASE, i32) + cast(digit_of(text[i]), i32);
        i += 1;
    }
    if (negative) {
        d->exponent = -exponent;
    } else {
        d->exponent = exponent;
    }
}

/// The next decimal of the same length upward: 1.24 after 1.23, and a power of
/// ten after all nines, where the digits collapse to a single one. That last
/// case is here for totality and no value can be printed by it: digits that are
/// all nines mean the value is within a hundredth of the power of ten above, so
/// that power of ten was the nearest one-digit decimal and was tried, and
/// rejected, at the very first length.
fn void step_up(decimal mut* d) {
    u64 mut i = d->digit_count;
    while (i > 0) {
        i -= 1;
        if (cast(d->digit[i], u64) < DECIMAL_BASE - 1) {
            d->digit[i] += 1;
            return;
        }
        d->digit[i] = 0;
    }
    d->digit[0] = 1;
    d->digit_count = 1;
    d->exponent += 1;
}

// ---- the layout ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// D18.3

/// Appends the exponent as `e`, a sign and at least two digits. The magnitude
/// is at most three digits, so the buffer of EXPONENT_MAX bytes it builds them
/// in has five to spare.
/// D11.7
fn u64 put_exponent(char mut@ out, u64 at, i32 exponent) {
    u64 mut n = at;
    out[n] = 'e';
    n += 1;
    u64 mut magnitude = cast(exponent, u64);
    if (exponent < 0) {
        out[n] = '-';
        magnitude = cast(-exponent, u64);
    } else {
        out[n] = '+';
    }
    n += 1;
    char[EXPONENT_MAX] mut text = {};
    u64 mut start = EXPONENT_MAX;
    u64 mut rest = magnitude;
    while (true) {
        start -= 1;
        text[start] = char_of(rest % DECIMAL_BASE);
        rest /= DECIMAL_BASE;
        if (rest == 0) {
            break;
        }
    }
    while (EXPONENT_MAX - start < EXP_MIN_DIGITS) {
        start -= 1;
        text[start] = '0';
    }
    while (start < EXPONENT_MAX) {
        out[n] = text[start];
        n += 1;
        start += 1;
    }
    return n;
}

/// Lays the digits out: exponent form below 1e-4 and at 1e17 and above, fixed
/// form between, with `.0` appended when the fixed text has neither a point nor
/// an exponent. `out` holds TEXT_MAX bytes; the result is how many were
/// written.
/// D18.3
fn u64 layout(bool negative, decimal* d, char mut@ out) {
    u64 mut n = 0;
    if (negative) {
        out[n] = '-';
        n += 1;
    }
    if (d->exponent < EXP_LOW || d->exponent >= EXP_HIGH) {
        out[n] = char_of(cast(d->digit[0], u64));
        n += 1;
        if (d->digit_count > 1) {
            out[n] = '.';
            n += 1;
            for (u64 mut i = 1; i < d->digit_count; i += 1) {
                out[n] = char_of(cast(d->digit[i], u64));
                n += 1;
            }
        }
        return put_exponent(out, n, d->exponent);
    }
    if (d->exponent < 0) {
        out[n] = '0';
        n += 1;
        out[n] = '.';
        n += 1;
        // The zeros between the point and the first digit.
        for (i32 mut i = -1; i > d->exponent; i -= 1) {
            out[n] = '0';
            n += 1;
        }
        for (u64 mut i = 0; i < d->digit_count; i += 1) {
            out[n] = char_of(cast(d->digit[i], u64));
            n += 1;
        }
        return n;
    }
    u64 integer_digits = cast(d->exponent, u64) + 1;
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < integer_digits; i += 1) {
        // The integer part is padded with zeros when the digits run out
        // before the point.
        if (i < d->digit_count) {
            out[n] = char_of(cast(d->digit[i], u64));
        } else {
            out[n] = '0';
        }
        n += 1;
    }
    out[n] = '.';
    n += 1;
    if (d->digit_count <= integer_digits) {
        out[n] = '0';
        n += 1;
        return n;
    }
    for (u64 mut i = integer_digits; i < d->digit_count; i += 1) {
        out[n] = char_of(cast(d->digit[i], u64));
        n += 1;
    }
    return n;
}

// ---- the search ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// D18.2

/// The decimal as a NUL-terminated text, laid out as it would print, which is
/// the text the reader is given.
fn void decimal_text(decimal* d, char mut@ buf) {
    u64 n = layout(false, d, buf);
    buf[n] = '\0';
}

/// Whether the decimal reads back as `v`, in each type. strtod and strtof are
/// correctly rounded down to the subnormals, where they also report ERANGE;
/// only the value matters here, so errno is left alone.
/// D18.2
fn bool reads_back_f64(decimal* d, f64 v) {
    char[TEXT_MAX_NUL] mut text = {};
    decimal_text(d, text[..]);
    return strtod(&text[0], null) == v;
}

fn bool reads_back_f32(decimal* d, f32 v) {
    char[TEXT_MAX_NUL] mut text = {};
    decimal_text(d, text[..]);
    return strtof(&text[0], null) == v;
}

/// The shortest decimal that reads back as `v` in f64, and of the texts of that
/// length the one nearest `v`; the module's header describes the search and why
/// the neighbour above is tried. `v` is a finite magnitude, never negative and
/// never a NaN.
/// D18.2
fn void decimal_f64(f64 v, decimal mut* d) {
    char[SCRATCH_MAX] mut scratch = {};
    for (u64 mut precision = 0; precision < DIGITS_F64; precision += 1) {
        scratch[0] = '\0';
        if (snprintf(&scratch[0], SCRATCH_MAX, FORMAT.ptr, cast(precision, i32), v) < 0) {
            // A printf that fails leaves the text empty, which scans as zero.
            scratch[0] = '\0';
        }
        scan(&scratch[0], d);
        if (precision == DIGITS_F64 - 1 || reads_back_f64(d, v)) {
            return;
        }
        decimal mut up = *d;
        step_up(&up);
        if (reads_back_f64(&up, v)) {
            *d = up;
            return;
        }
    }
}

/// The same search in f32, where 9 digits always read back and strtof decides.
/// The magnitude widens to f64 for snprintf without changing value.
/// D18.2: the shortest decimal that reads back in its own type
fn void decimal_f32(f32 v, decimal mut* d) {
    char[SCRATCH_MAX] mut scratch = {};
    for (u64 mut precision = 0; precision < DIGITS_F32; precision += 1) {
        scratch[0] = '\0';
        if (snprintf(&scratch[0], SCRATCH_MAX, FORMAT.ptr, cast(precision, i32),
                     cast(v, f64)) < 0) {
            scratch[0] = '\0';
        }
        scan(&scratch[0], d);
        if (precision == DIGITS_F32 - 1 || reads_back_f32(d, v)) {
            return;
        }
        decimal mut up = *d;
        step_up(&up);
        if (reads_back_f32(&up, v)) {
            *d = up;
            return;
        }
    }
}

// ---- the entry points ----------------------------------------------------------------
// D18.1

/// The sign bit of the value: -0.0 == 0.0, so a comparison cannot tell the two
/// apart and the bytes decide. std.math will hold this bit cast when it exists
/// (stdlib.md 4); until then it is a memcpy, which is what stdlib.md's own
/// sketch of f64_bits does.
/// D6.12
fn bool negative_f64(f64 v) {
    u64 mut bits = 0;
    f64 mut source = v;
    libc.memcpy(cast(&bits, u8 mut*), cast(&source, u8*), F64_BYTES);
    return (bits >> F64_SIGN_SHIFT) != 0;
}

fn bool negative_f32(f32 v) {
    u32 mut bits = 0;
    f32 mut source = v;
    libc.memcpy(cast(&bits, u8 mut*), cast(&source, u8*), F32_BYTES);
    return (bits >> F32_SIGN_SHIFT) != 0;
}

/// The text of a value in `out`, and how many bytes it is: everything
/// print_f64 does but the writing. It stands apart from the entry point so
/// that the bytes can be held against a table without a descriptor, which is
/// what run/stdlib/094 does; `out` holds TEXT_MAX bytes.
fn u64 text_f64(f64 v, char mut@ out) {
    if (v != v) {
        // The print family lists inf, -inf and nan and no -nan, so a NaN
        // prints the same whatever its sign bit.
        // D11.7
        return fixed_text(out, "nan");
    }
    bool negative = negative_f64(v);
    if (v > F64_MAX || v < -F64_MAX) {
        // Every finite f64 is at most F64_MAX in magnitude, so a larger one is
        // an infinity.
        if (negative) {
            return fixed_text(out, "-inf");
        }
        return fixed_text(out, "inf");
    }
    f64 mut magnitude = v;
    if (negative) {
        magnitude = -v;
    }
    decimal mut d = decimal{};
    decimal_f64(magnitude, &d);
    return layout(negative, &d, out);
}

/// The same for an f32. An f32 is never widened to an f64 first, because the
/// digits printed depend on the argument's own type.
/// D18.1
fn u64 text_f32(f32 v, char mut@ out) {
    if (v != v) {
        return fixed_text(out, "nan");
    }
    bool negative = negative_f32(v);
    if (v > F32_MAX || v < -F32_MAX) {
        if (negative) {
            return fixed_text(out, "-inf");
        }
        return fixed_text(out, "inf");
    }
    f32 mut magnitude = v;
    if (negative) {
        magnitude = -v;
    }
    decimal mut d = decimal{};
    decimal_f32(magnitude, &d);
    return layout(negative, &d, out);
}

/// One of the three texts the print family fixes, copied into the caller's
/// buffer.
/// D11.7
fn u64 fixed_text(char mut@ out, string text) {
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < text.len) {
        out[i] = text[i];
        i += 1;
    }
    return text.len;
}

/// Writes the text of an f64 to the buffer of `fd`.
/// D11.7, D18.1
fn void print_f64(i32 fd, f64 v) {
    char[TEXT_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 n = text_f64(v, out[..]);
    rt.emit(fd, cast(out[0..n], u8@));
}

/// The same for an f32.
fn void print_f32(i32 fd, f32 v) {
    char[TEXT_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 n = text_f32(v, out[..]);
    rt.emit(fd, cast(out[0..n], u8@));
}

/// Appends the text of an f64 to `b` (stdlib.md 2.12). It is print_f64 with a
/// buffer in place of the descriptor: both take their bytes from text_f64, so
/// the two forms cannot disagree over a value. This is why the module imports
/// std.strbuf, and why the buffer form stands here and not in std.strbuf, which
/// no float may reach.
/// D18.1
fn void append_f64(strbuf.str_buf mut* b, f64 v) {
    char[TEXT_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 n = text_f64(v, out[..]);
    strbuf.append_bytes(b, cast(out[0..n], u8@));
}

/// The same for an f32, whose digits are its own type's.
/// D18.1
fn void append_f32(strbuf.str_buf mut* b, f32 v) {
    char[TEXT_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 n = text_f32(v, out[..]);
    strbuf.append_bytes(b, cast(out[0..n], u8@));
}
